🔹 Task 1 — Access Modifiers & Valid Overrides

Create two classes: Animal and Dog.

In Animal, create three methods:

public void eat()

protected void makeSound()

void sleep() (package-private)

In Dog, attempt to override all three.

For each override, make the access modifier:

valid for one method

invalid (too restrictive) for another

same for the last

Observe which ones compile and which do not, and understand why.

🔹 Task 2 — Checked Exceptions & Overriding

Create a class Parent with:

public void process() throws IOException


Then create a Child class.

In Child, try these variants of overriding process():

Throwing a narrower checked exception (e.g., FileNotFoundException)

Throwing no exception

Throwing a broader exception (e.g., Exception)

Identify which versions are valid and which cause compilation errors.

🔹 Task 3 — Static Method Hiding vs. Overriding

Create a class A with:

public static void identify()


and a class B that extends A, also declaring:

public static void identify()


Then write:

A a1 = new A();
A a2 = new B();
B b1 = new B();


Call identify() on each variable:

a1.identify();
a2.identify();
b1.identify();


Predict and verify the output.
This will help you see the difference between method hiding (static) and method overriding (instance).

🔹 Task 4 — Variable Hiding

Create:

class SuperClass {
    public String name = "Super";
}

class SubClass extends SuperClass {
    public String name = "Sub";
}


Then:

SuperClass s1 = new SuperClass();
SuperClass s2 = new SubClass();
SubClass s3 = new SubClass();


Print name for each reference.
Explain why methods use dynamic dispatch, but variables use reference type, not object type.